as shown significant recovery with 80 species of birds, 22 species of fish, and 10 species of invertebrates observed. The project aimed to restore natural tidal flows and enhance habitat diversity. Native plants began emerging quickly after the restoration, indicating ecological recovery.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2011-california-completed-a-300-acre-restoration-of-former-san-diego-salt-ponds-five-year-monitoring-later-documented-80-bird-22-fish-and-10-invertebrate-species/articleshow/134692067.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2011-california-completed-a-300-acre-restoration-of-former-san-diego-salt-ponds-five-year-monitoring-later-documented-80-bird-22-fish-and-10-invertebrate-species/articleshow/134692067.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-10-06T10:21:24+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-134693647,imgsize-854750.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Chocolate reached east of Mississippi 1,000 years ago; ancient pottery reveals cacao at feasts]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/134688736.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[Research conducted at the Etowah Indian Mounds has revealed cacao residue in 11th-century pottery, illustrating that the Mississippian culture actively consumed cacao as part of their lifestyle. This discovery suggests interconnected trade networks similar to those seen in Chaco Canyon, hinting at shared cultural exchanges. The distinct pottery styles from Etowah further emphasize this cultural interaction, prompting further investigation into these ancient societies.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/archaeology/ancient-pottery-reveals-earliest-known-traces-of-chocolate-in-east-of-mississ